 sort of  short-term  shortfall of of energy in part because  of the economic rebound from the  pandemic and so we have to recognize  that there are some short-term  challenges that we face and not lose  sight of the longer-term challenge  because in the end regardless of what  happens to oil prices and and uh fossil  fuel energy prices right now  in the end uh it will be far cheaper for  us to move in the direction of renewable  energy  it's better for the economy it's better  for the planet and so we have to  continue to decarbonize our economy we  have to continue to work towards global  negotiations to reduce carbon emissions  dramatically over the next decade or so  and that means no new fossil fuel  infrastructure even the conservative iea  has said that if we are to stabilize  warming below truly catastrophic levels  of more than one and a half degrees  celsius  we cannot invest in any new fossil fuel  infrastructure any new pipelines oil  natural gas pipelines coal mines and so  we have to keep that in mind even as we  deal with the immediate challenges that  we face okay let's move on to our next  topic shall we because  9.2 trillion dollars a year that's how  much the world needs to spend every year  in order to reach net zero and maintain  a habitable world according to new  analysis by mckinsey consultancy it  sounds like a lot but it is and if you  consider climate change to be is it  really that much if you consider climate  change to be an existential crisis now  if you've seen the film don't look up  you'll know that one of the points it  makes is that an obsession with money  ultimately well no spoilers but it  doesn't end well for them so bernice a  fundamental transformation of the world  economy and nine trillion dollars should  be spent annually in investment i mean  it's it's a huge amount it's a big ask  is it pie in the sky will it happen do  you think  it is a huge ask but at the same time i  think mckenzie has pointed out that  actually the cost of doing nothing would  be much much higher and so in some sense  it is in the context of much larger cost  of the economy and i think all the  studies we've looked at we're talking  about 10 23  you know the color cost of impacts and  that actually means that it will be cost  something a lot more than what covert is  currently costing us for example and  also i think the good news as well or  the hope at least is that because  historically all these modeling has  always underestimated how quickly  renewables have gotten cheaper in the  past so there is great hope that  actually we will get a lot cheaper and  therefore the cost is less than what  this estimate would say so on the one  side impacts cost far more  on the other side actually hopefully if  the numbers are you know if the cost of  the fast cost of reduction is  underestimated on the other side there  is a great chance that actually we will  come on inside a lot more balanced than  these numbers necessarily imply  michael do you agree with that analysis  and ultimately who's going to be  responsible for this transformation and  this investment are we talking about  governments are we talking about  corporations  well it's all of the above right we have  to work together um the the investment  uh world uh uh governments um  individuals uh it's all hands on deck  and i would uh absolutely uh reiterate  the central points that bernice just  made first of all when it comes to the  cost of action  this report i haven't looked at it in  detail yet but it probably relies for  example on the iea projections of  renewable energy costs and the iea has  dramatically  underestimated  the um the the lowering in uh cost of  renewable energy in past years uh over  the past decade they have consistently  therefore overestimated the cost of the  transition to renewable energy and if  this report for example uses those  numbers and i suspect it does then it is  overestimating how much it will cost  to decarbonize our economy  at the same time  there are all of these hidden costs that  aren't entirely taken into account in  many of these sorts of estimates uh the  hidden cost of our reliance on fossil  fuels we already talked about the uh  national security  challenges that we face um the the  the cost of maintaining a military to  fight battles um in foreign lands to  maintain access to fossil fuels and the  health cost and and you know there was a  segment earlier in the program about  beijing and and the health um you know  the health challenges that they are  facing because of their continued  reliance on dirty energy and so when we  take all those into account i suspect  the cost of inaction  is even greater than implied in the  mckinsey report but as bernice said even  given mckinsey's very conservative  numbers it's very clear that the cost of  inaction is far greater than the cost of  taking action and that's all the more  reason to accelerate our efforts  domestically  here in the united states in europe and  our efforts diplomatically uh when we  return to the table uh next year in the  one year follow-up to the glasgow  conference there's an opportunity to  really ratchet up those commitments and  bernice then briefly how do you  communicate the message either  internationally and diplomatically but  also to consumers as well that the money  needs to be spent  look i mean it will cost a lot more if  we don't spend it now a lot of it is fun  loaded there's also you know as we are  now increasingly realizing the cost of  the impacts are coming through earlier  than we think and therefore it is quite  important that we invest now and the  action time is really now rather than  later  okay well bernice lee and michael mann  always interesting to get your thoughts  thanks so much for for joining us  tonight  and just let you know that tomorrow i'll  be joined by the director and founder of  uplift tessa khan and policy director at  the grantham ins research institute on  climate change and the environment bob  ward but for now thanks for watching  i'll see you next time  [Music]
 

